    "evidence_text": "Memory and Modularity in Cell-Fate Decision Making\n\nGenetically identical cells sharing an environment can display markedly different phenotypes.\n\nIn contrast, the time spent as connected chains of cells is tightly controlled, enforcing coordination among related cells in the multicellular state.\n\nThe transcriptional program of sporulation in budding yeast\n\nDNA microarrays containing nearly every yeast gene were used to assay changes in gene expression during sporulation.\n\nAt least seven distinct temporal patterns of induction were observed.\n\nmicroRNAs: A Safeguard against Turmoil?\n\nSurprisingly, miRNAs, which normally suppress expression of target transcripts, may become activators of expression during stress.\n\nThe differentiation of the bacterium Bacillus subtilis into a dormant spore is among the most well-characterized developmental pathways in biology.\n\nMore recently, transcriptional profiling uncovered additional sporulation-induced genes required for successful spore development.\n\nOur screen identified 133 out of the 148 genes with known sporulation defects.\n\nThis analysis identified mutants that are delayed in the initiation of sporulation, defective in membrane remodeling, and impaired in spore maturation.\n\nSeveral mutants had novel sporulation phenotypes.\n\nWe performed in-depth characterization of two new factors that participate in cell-cell signaling pathways during sporulation.\n\nFinally, we discovered a large set of transposon insertions that trigger premature initiation of sporulation.\n\nMafB/c-Maf deficiency enables self-renewal of differentiated functional macrophages.\n\nIt thus appears possible to amplify functional differentiated cells without malignant transformation or stem cell intermediates.\n\nCell survival during complete nutrient deprivation depends on lipid droplet-fueled \u03b2-oxidation of fatty acids.\n\nGenotoxic Stress Abrogates Renewal of Melanocyte Stem Cells by Triggering Their Differentiation\n\nThe resulting MSC depletion leads to irreversible hair graying.\n\nSelf-renewal and differentiation of stem cells are tightly regulated processes subject to intrinsic and extrinsic signals.\n\nIt appears that embryonic stem cells exhibit increased stress tolerance and concomitant high levels of chaperone expression.\n\nThis review critically evaluates stem cell research from a molecular chaperone perspective.\n\nPurification and characterization of mouse hematopoietic stem cells.\n\nThese cells can proliferate and differentiate with approximately unit efficiency into myelomonocytic cells, B cells, or T cells.\n\nModeling the evolution of culture-adapted human embryonic stem cells.\n\nHowever, the evolutionary influences of population size, mutation rate, and selection pressure are frequently overlooked.\n\nIn particular, it highlights the effect of population size, suggesting that the maintenance of cells in small populations reduces the likelihood that abnormal cultures will develop.",
